UnixLib's inline SWI wrappers (incl-local/internal/os.h) declared their results as local register variables (register T x __asm ("r1")) and read them in C after the asm.  GCC guarantees
such a variable to be in its register only where it is an operand of an asm; everywhere else the variable IS the hard register, and a call between the asm and the read has overwritten it.
GCC 16 at -O2 did that to SWI_DDEUtils_GetCLSize in __unixinit: arg_size became the result of the strlen () that follows it, so with the DDEUtils module loaded (StrongED, !Zap and !Edit load it)
every program started with arguments longer than its own name got its arguments cut to the length of the name, in a heap block too small for them (and the SOManager loader hands the arguments
over in the DDEUtils command line whenever the module is there).  Every wrapper now captures its outputs in the asm itself into ordinary variables (an early clobber MOV at the end of
the template), the input register variables that the SWI changes are in-out operands, and the changed registers that are not operands are clobbers.  OS_GBPB 2 and 4, which advance the
buffer address in R2, now say so.  Generated by tools/fix-unixlib-regvars.py from the pristine file.
